  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 435 AM EDT TUE AUG 18 2026\n\nBy Wednesday night, the cold frontal boundary will sag closer to \nthe area and shortwaves riding through the broader upper level\ntroughing will aid in forcing convection. This will lead to an \nincreasing threat of showers and thunderstorms Wednesday night, \nwith chances peaking in the 50 to 60 percent range near/north of \nI-64 and lesser chances (20 to 30 percent range) toward the \nVirginia border. The chance of showers and thunderstorms then \nincreases further Thursday (50 to 70 percent range), as the \nfrontal boundary sags deeper into Kentucky and a more pronounced \nshortwave arrives. There is a decent surge of moisture noted with \nthe boundary, with PWAT values generally peaking in the 1.7 to 1.9\ninch range, but the overall movement of this activity is expected\nto be rather progressive. Even so, locations that experience \nmultiple rounds of convection could be at risk of minor flooding \nissues, and WPC does have all of eastern Kentucky in a Marginal \nRisk (1 out of 5) for Thursday into Thursday night. There is at \nleast some question on the potential for a few stronger storms, \nwith MUCAPE in the 1000-2000 J/kg range and effective shear in the\n30-40 kt range. This is a reasonable enough parameter space to \naid in organizing convection, with the main risk being strong wind\ngusts. This comes as DCAPE values increase toward 900 J/kg \nThursday afternoon. \n\nBy the end of the week, the cold front is expected to slowly sink\nsouthward. There is some uncertainty as to how far, but enough to\nkeep rain chances lower for Friday and Saturday, generally in the\nslight chance/chance range. Then by the late weekend into early \nnext week there remains at least some model uncertainty with \nregard to the depth of the eastern CONUS trough as stronger\nshortwave drops out of the Northwest Canadian Arctic. Some \nguidance shows a closed upper low developing across the northern \nOhio Valley/Eastern Great Lakes while 500 hPA ridging pumps back \nup over the Western CONUS. This could set the stage for a Canadian\ncold front to cross eastern Kentucky late Saturday or early Sunday\nwith a subsequent early fall-like high pressure system to start \nthe last full week of August.",
